amentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Illinois enters the presidential race as a Democratic-leaning state, with a partisan lean of about six points toward Democrats and no incumbent on the ballot after the term-limited president. The Democratic ticket is favored, but the expected margin is modest by statewide standards and leaves room for a competitive contest if the national climate breaks against the party.\n\nThe evidence base is thin: no general-election polls, expert ratings, or market pricing are available, so the forecast rests on the state's underlying partisan balance. Recent headlines point to continued Democratic organization in Illinois, including wins in the 2026 midterms and active speculation about Governor Pritzker's national future, though none of it quantifies a shift in the state's lean. Uncertainty is wider than usual, and the outlook would move quickly with the first credible statewide surveys.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7608, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.746, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7069, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.7035, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.7139, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.7208, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.7158, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.714, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.7198, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.727, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.7103, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.733, "margin": 6.0}]}